  "query_preview": "..\n\nWinds will diminish this morning as skies clear and the \natmosphere decouples. Previous rain showers may produce enough low\nlevel moisture for some isolated patchy fog along the\nInternational Border in the north. Then a warm front moves east \nthrough later today with some clouds associated with it. High \ntemperatures will be about 5 to 10 degrees warmer than the \nprevious days.\n\nA warmer day is expected Thursday partially due to compressional\nwarming as a cold front approaches from the northwest. Temps\nshould reach up to the 80s in most places or 5 to 15 degrees above\nnormal. With increasing west winds, minimum humidity in the teens\nand little rainfall in the southeast zones recently a Fire \nWeather Watch was issued for Thursday from noon till 8 pm. This\nwatch was expanded to southern Petroleum and Garfield counties. \n\nThe cold front then drops southeast across the area Thursday night\nbringing the chance of light showers Thursday night into Friday \nmorning. \n\nThe cooler/wetter conditions linger through the weekend as an\nsecond heavier wave of rains moves in along an upper trough from \nthe Pacific NW. Temps by Sunday will drop to 10 to 20 below\nseasonal norms with this passage as well. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nDeviations from NBM include hourly Visibility into the early\nmorning today which was tweaked downward for light fog across the\nInternational Border.\n\nConfidence in temps is HIGH through Saturday.\n\nConfidence in temps is MODERATE afterward.",
